<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guangdong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guangdong is a coastal province of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, and the most populous province of the country. Part of the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a>, it was a centre of maritime trade for over a thousand years, the base of the Southern Han kingdom in the 10th century and the gateway for European trade under the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-system">Canton System</a>. Since 1980 special economic zones such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a> have made it China's largest provincial economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guangdong.jpg" alt="Guangdong" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Resources_Headquarters%26Shenzhen_Bay_gymnasium_in_Nanshan_District2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liangzhu was extremely influential: its sphere of influence reached as far north as Shanxi and as far south as <b>Guangdong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1837 Hong Huoxiu, a Hakka from a poor village in <b>Guangdong</b>, failed the imperial examination for the third time and suffered a breakdown, in which he dreamed of visiting Heaven and meeting a celestial family; his heavenly father gave him the name Hong Xiuquan.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Mac Cuu, a Ming loyalist from <b>Guangdong</b>, settled at Ha Tien, then a Cambodian coastal town called Peam, in the late 17th century and developed it into a trading port with Cambodian approval.</blockquote><a href="/state/ha-tien-principality">Ha Tien Principality</a></li><li><blockquote>Luo Fangbo, a Hakka scholar and migrant from <b>Guangdong</b>, organised Chinese gold miners near Pontianak and in 1777 founded the Lanfang kongsi at Mandor.</blockquote><a href="/state/lanfang-republic">Lanfang Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern Han controlled modern <b>Guangdong</b> and Guangxi and grew rich on the sea trade with South-east Asia and the Indian Ocean, handled by Arab, Persian and other foreign merchants at Guangzhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a></li><li><blockquote>Hong Xiuquan, a Hakka from <b>Guangdong</b> who repeatedly failed the civil service examinations, came to believe from visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of Jesus.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiping-heavenly-kingdom">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
